        <h1>Instrumentation</h1>


        <!-- H6/7 ARTIFACT -->

        <div class="artifact-card">

            <h2>Artifact: H6/7 Sickle Scaler</h2>

            <div class="artifact-flex">

                <!-- LEFT SIDE: DESCRIPTION AND REFLECTION -->

                <div class="artifact-content">

                    <div class="instrument-description">

                        <h3>Instrument Description</h3>

                        <p>
                            The H6/7 is an anterior sickle scaler used
                            for supragingival calculus removal on
                            anterior teeth, including facial, lingual,
                            and interproximal surfaces. It has a sharp,
                            pointed tip and a 90-degree face-to-lower-shank
                            design. Because of its pointed tip, the
                            instrument is inserted at the line angle
                            rather than the midline to avoid
                            traumatizing the surrounding soft tissue.
                        </p>

                    </div>


                    <h3>Reflection:</h3>

                    <div class="reflection-text">

                        <p>
                            During my simulated H6/7 test, I struggled
                            mainly with ergonomics, grasp, and speed.
                            I had difficulty positioning the client's
                            head without bending my wrists, and my
                            instrument would sometimes slip past my
                            second knuckle. I was also working too
                            quickly, which affected my control.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            My clinician recommended slowing down and
                            raising the client chair. During a later
                            Gracey 1/2 demonstration, I learned that
                            the client's jaw should be aligned with
                            my belly button, depending on whether
                            I was working on the maxillary or
                            mandibular arch.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Once I corrected the client positioning,
                            slowed down, and maintained my grasp,
                            I was able to fix the issues I had
                            experienced during the simulation.
                            The H6/7 became the instrument I felt
                            most comfortable using because I finally
                            understood what I had been doing wrong
                            and how to correct it. Using the
                            1-2-3 pivot technique also helped me
                            maintain a controlled pace and prevent
                            slipping during my strokes.
                        </p>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- RIGHT SIDE: H6/7 PHOTO -->

                <div class="artifact-images">

                    <div class="artifact-photo">
                        <img src="~/h67.png"
                             alt="H6/7 Instrumentation Artifact">
                    </div>

                </div>

            </div>

        </div>



        <!-- 204S ARTIFACT -->

        <div class="artifact-card">

            <h2>Artifact: 204S Sickle Scaler</h2>

            <div class="artifact-flex">

                <!-- LEFT SIDE: DESCRIPTION AND REFLECTION -->

                <div class="artifact-content">

                    <div class="instrument-description">

                        <h3>Instrument Description</h3>

                        <p>
                            The 204S is a posterior sickle scaler
                            used for supragingival calculus removal,
                            particularly on the interproximal
                            surfaces of posterior teeth.
                            It has a triangular cross-section,
                            a sharp pointed tip, two cutting edges
                            on each working end, and a 90-degree
                            face-to-lower-shank relationship.
                        </p>

                    </div>


                    <h3>Reflection:</h3>

                    <div class="reflection-text">

                        <p>
                            During my simulated 204S test, I struggled
                            with maintaining a stable fulcrum,
                            identifying the correct working end
                            for quadrant one buccal, and client
                            positioning. My fulcrum positioning
                            was particularly difficult because
                            I was not always resting on the
                            correct part of my finger.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            My clinician pointed out that the client
                            chair was too low and recommended
                            positioning the client at tummy level.
                            I was also advised to stand up more
                            to improve my fulcrum, make smaller
                            hand movements, and ensure that the
                            instrument was in contact at the
                            corner of the tooth before pivoting.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            By the time my test came around,
                            I had corrected almost all of the
                            challenges from my simulation except
                            for my fulcrum. After attending
                            skills development and practising,
                            I realized that I needed to make
                            a slight adjustment to my position.
                            This helped me correct my fulcrum
                            and improve my control of the instrument.
                        </p>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <!-- RIGHT SIDE: 204S PHOTO -->

                <div class="artifact-images">

                    <div class="artifact-images">

                        <div class="artifact-photo">
                            <img src="~/204s.png"
                                 alt="204s Instrumentation Artifact">
                        </div>

                </div>

            </div>

        </div>

